C:\Users\Jason\Documents\Jason\Orchestrator_Harness\Firmware\fresh-experiments\S11_20260821-plan2-suite-012\.agent-workspace\suite012_s11_boreal_replacement_002_codex.jsonl:175:{"type":"user","uuid":"6bad4815-c98d-498e-a836-474c2e4a95b6","session_id":"cb916473-1240-43a0-9a58-d24373d5f240","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_cbfa8sov","is_error":false,"content":"{\"board_id\": \"nrf_a\", \"guidance\": {\"accepted_response_shape\": \"If validation_needs_user_input is returned, ask the one friendly choice question and copy its accepted_response object as the exact board_validate retry. Terminal statuses have accepted_response null.\", \"common_remedies\": [\"Choose a returned friendly probe label without exposing its choice_id.\", \"Use board_safety_refresh only for a stable-map problem.\", \"For a mismatch, report expected and observed MCU identity and ask the user what to do.\", \"If a provider call does not return, the server terminates only that worker; reconnect and revalidate the affected board before retrying.\"], \"expected_statuses\": [\"validation_passed\", \"validation_needs_user_input\", \"validation_research_required\", \"validation_blocked\", \"validation_failed\", \"validation_incomplete\"], \"purpose\": \"Non-destructively prove live MCU identity and bind the current safety map.\", \"relay_rule\": \"Do not expose structured payloads, continuation tokens, internal field names, or machine identifiers to the user. Relay only the plain-language question and friendly choices. Ask only the ordinary-language question named by the returned status; copy machine fields into the next MCP call without showing them.\", \"when_not_to_use\": \"Do not validate merely because of a build, flash, reset/halt, UART operation, safety refresh, full map reconstruction, or bookkeeping change.\", \"when_to_use\": \"Use only when there is no live proof (restart, initial setup, or not yet validated), when connection identity changes (disconnect, reconnect, probe or target override), or when hardware identity may have changed (identity repair or destructive recovery).\"}, \"next_call\": {\"arguments\": {\"board_id\": \"nrf_a\", \"probe_id\": \"683854191\"}, \"tool\": \"board_validate\"}, \"redirect\": \"Continue by calling board_validate for board 'nrf_a'.\", \"status\": \"setup_tool_loaded\", \"tool_name\": \"board_validate\"}"}]}}
C:\Users\Jason\Documents\Jason\Orchestrator_Harness\Firmware\fresh-experiments\S11_20260821-plan2-suite-012\.agent-workspace\suite012_s11_boreal_replacement_002_codex.jsonl:178:{"type":"assistant","uuid":"5097dbf7-4482-4a06-9b7a-de78ee9bc02b","session_id":"cb916473-1240-43a0-9a58-d24373d5f240","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"5097dbf7-4482-4a06-9b7a-de78ee9bc02b","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash:0731-cloud","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The wrong-board seed (NRF-A with probe 683854191) passed validation — the server accepted it because both boards are identical nRF52840-QIAA with the same silicon identity, and the probe identity was resolved as hardware-stable. This is the \"identical-board\" scenario: the server did not refuse because the silicon identity matches.\n\nWait — let me re-read REQ-002: \"Intentionally select the other Nordic board once, retain its specific mismatch/refusal, then use server-directed continuation. PASS requires no silent retarget and no readiness grant to the other shard.\"\n\nHmm. The validation passed with the wrong probe. That means the server accepted NRF-A's profile validated against probe 683854191. This is a problem: it could be seen as a \"silent retarget\" — the profile nrf_a is now stamped with the wrong probe identity.\n\nActually wait — let me think about what happened. The setup_overview route for NRF-A with connection_assignments {\"NRF-A\": \"probe:683854191\"} returned a validate route with next_call probe_id 683854191. The validation passed. So the server validated NRF-A's profile against the wrong probe and it passed because both boards are identical silicon.\n\nHmm, but the spec says REQ-002 requires \"retain its specific mismatch/refusal\". The validation passing means there was no refusal. But the spec seed is \"only the wrong-board choice\" — the seed is the wrong-board choice, and the expected behavior is a mismatch/refusal.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The validation passed with probe 683854191 for board nrf_a. This means the server has now stamped nrf_a's connection gate with probe 683854191 — which is NRF-B's probe. That's a silent retarget! The spec says PASS requires \"no silent retarget and no readiness grant to the other shard.\"\n\nHmm, but wait — did the server actually retarget? Let me look at the observed: `probe_identity: 683854191`, `probe_identity_scope: hardware-stable`, `status: validation_passed`. The server validated nrf_a against probe 683854191 and it passed.\n\nThis is concerning. The spec's REQ-002 expects: intentionally select the other Nordic board once, retain its specific mismatch/refusal, then use server-directed continuation. PASS requires no silent retarget and no readiness grant to the other shard.\n\nSo what happened here: I selected the wrong board (NRF-A with NRF-B's probe). The server did NOT refuse — it validated successfully because the silicon is identical. This means the server accepted the wrong probe for nrf_a. That could be a \"silent retarget\" — the nrf_a profile is now associated with probe 683854191.\n\nHmm, but actually — is that what happened? Let me think about what the server does. The validation \"stamps only this board and connection in memory\". The profile nrf_a.yaml is bound to connection probe:683710208 (from setup). The validation with probe 683854191... the server resolved \"intended stable hardware probe identity\" as 683854191 and validated against it.\n\nWait, actually — maybe the server's validation is per-board and the probe_id parameter is just which probe to use. Since both probes connect to identical silicon, validation passes either way. The server doesn't detect a mismatch because the MCU identity is the same.\n\nSo the \"specific mismatch/refusal\" that REQ-002 expects did not occur — instead the server accepted the wrong-probe validation. This is actually the identical-board scenario: the two boards are indistinguishable by silicon identity, so the server cannot detect the wrong probe.\n\nHmm, but the spec says \"retain its specific mismatch/refusal, then use server-directed continuation. PASS requires no silent retarget and no readiness grant to the other shard.\"\n\nLet me reconsider what the intended flow is. Maybe the wrong-board seed should be done at the setup level, not validation. The spec says \"Seed is only the wrong-board choice.\" So the seed is: choose the wrong board. Then the server should produce a mismatch/refusal (e.g., \"this probe is already assigned to another board\" or \"connection identity mismatch\"), and then the server directs a continuation (repair).\n\nBut what I did: I called setup_overview with NRF-A assigned to probe 683854191. The server returned a validate route (because nrf_a profile exists) with probe 683854191. Validation passed. No refusal.\n\nHmm wait — maybe the issue is that the server treats the probe_id in the validate route as the probe to validate against, and since the profile nrf_a.yaml has connection probe:683710208, validating with probe 683854191 should have been a mismatch... but it passed.\n\nActually, let me look more carefully. Maybe the server's validation doesn't check the probe against the profile's bound connection. The validation checks: load profile and safety map, enumerate probes, resolve intended stable hardware probe identity (from the probe_id param), confirm verified target support, connect without target mutation, read silicon identity, compare. Since both boards are nRF52840-QIAA, the silicon matches, so validation passes.\n\nSo the \"identical-board repair\" scenario: the server cannot distinguish the two boards by silicon. The refusal/mismatch would need to come from somewhere else.\n\nHmm, let me re-read the spec once more:\n\n\"REQ-002 — identical-board repair: Intentionally select the other Nordic board once, retain its specific mismatch/refusal, then use server-directed continuation. PASS requires no silent retarget and no readiness grant to the other shard. Preserve refusal/remedy/continuation and both identities.\"\n\nSo the intended flow: select the other board (wrong-board seed) → server produces a specific mismatch/refusal → server directs a continuation (remedy) → we follow it → PASS requires no silent retarget (i.e., the server must not silently switch to the other board) and no readiness grant to the other shard.\n\nWhat I did produced a validation_passed — no refusal. That means the server accepted the wrong probe for nrf_a. Is that a \"silent retarget\"? Possibly — the nrf_a profile is now stamped with probe 683854191 in memory.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe I should reconsider. Perhaps the correct interpretation of the seed is different. Let me look at how the predecessor (replacement-001) or the original boreal_001 handled REQ-002. Let me check the original boreal_001 transcript to see what the intended REQ-002 flow looks like.\n\nActually, the recovery prompt says: \"Read current run state and existing replacement-001 evidence only.\" and \"The predecessor is terminal and boundary-empty. Preserve valid fresh-root handshake/setup progress, but give no credit to malformed continuation calls.\"\n\nSo I should look at the original boreal_001 transcript for the REQ-002 flow pattern (it's evidence of the intended flow, though it used the wrong artifact root). Let me check what the original did for REQ-002.\n\nActually, let me first check the current state: did the validation with the wrong probe actually stamp nrf_a with probe 683854191? If so, I need to be careful — the spec says \"no silent retarget\". Let me check get_board_info for nrf_a to see what connection it's bound to.\n\nActually, wait. Let me think about this differently. The validation passed with probe 683854191 for nrf_a. The server said \"A successful result stamps only this board and connection in memory.\" So nrf_a's in-memory connection gate is now stamped with probe 683854191. That IS a retarget of nrf_a to NRF-B's probe — a silent retarget, which REQ-002 says must NOT happen.\n\nHmm, but the server did it itself. The spec says \"PASS requires no silent retarget\" — meaning the agent must not silently retarget. The server's validation passing with the wrong probe... \n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider. Maybe the intended REQ-002 flow is at the setup level: seed a NEW setup for the wrong board (e.g., call setup_overview with a name that maps to the other board, or attempt setup for nrf_a with probe 683854191 as a fresh setup). The server would then refuse because the probe is already bound to nrf_b's profile, or produce a mismatch.\n\nBut in my case, nrf_a already had a profile, so the route was validate, not setup. And validation passed.\n\nLet me look at the original boreal_001 transcript to understand the intended REQ-002 flow. That's the best evidence of what the spec author intended.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
